adjudication.rationale:
In `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`, `minimumTokenResponseSchema` is defined as:
```ts
const minimumTokenResponseSchema = z.object({
  access_token: z.string(),
  [z.string().toString()]: z.number(),
  [z.string().optional().toString()]: z.unknown().optional(),
});
```
Those computed keys are evaluated immediately to a literal string (e.g. "ZodString" / "[object Object]") rather than acting as a wildcard for “any string key”. This makes the schema require a specific property with that literal name to exist and be a number, which normal OAuth token responses will not contain, so `safeParse(response)` will fail whenever this schema is used. Additionally, `z.object` strips unknown keys by default, so even if it didn’t fail, it would not preserve arbitrary expiry fields. The finding correctly identifies a real defect introduced by the diff.
